<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tang of Shang</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cheng Tang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tang of Shang, also called Cheng Tang, was the founder of the Shang dynasty according to Chinese tradition. Histories such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a> relate that he overthrew Jie, the tyrannical last king of the Xia, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mingtiao">Battle of Mingtiao</a>, with the help of his minister Yi Yin. Oracle-bone inscriptions honour him as Da Yi, an important founding ancestor of the Shang kings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tang-of-shang.jpg" alt="Tang of Shang" width="320" height="704" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ma Lin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Tang_of_Shang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tang was born Zi Lü and is named Da Yi or Tai Yi on oracle bones, where the character yi marks the day on which sacrifices were made to him. Cheng Tang is a posthumous title. He was said to descend from Xie, and so from Emperor Ku and the Yellow Emperor. Because his time precedes the Late Shang, records of his life are limited to oracle bone inscriptions and works written after his death.</p><p>Tang succeeded Shi Gui as ruler of the Predynastic Shang, one of many kingdoms under the suzerainty of the Xia. His minister Yi Yin is said to have won his confidence through cooking, advising on the balance of the five flavours before giving political advice. Yi Yin spent about three years as an envoy at the Xia capital and came back critical of Xia politics. He urged war but held that allies must first be won and Xia's vassals weakened.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In the 21st year of Jie's reign Tang took the Youluo and forced the Jing to surrender. The next year Jie imprisoned him at Xiatai but later released him, and many neighbouring clans came to revere Shang. Tang is said to have won the support of as many as 40 smaller kingdoms by pointing to Jie's mistreatment of his people, telling them that he must follow the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandate-of-heaven">Mandate of Heaven</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-documents">Book of Documents</a> contains a speech attributed to him after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mingtiao">Battle of Mingtiao</a>, though its authenticity is disputed.</p><p>The Shang army defeated Jie's forces at Mingtiao in a heavy thunderstorm. Jie fled to Sanzong, was pursued to Cheng by the Shang general Wuzi, and was captured at Jiaomen and deposed. Tang succeeded him as paramount king. According to the Bamboo Annals, when drought struck in the first five years of his reign he had golden coins made so that poor families could buy back children sold in the famine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In oracle bone inscriptions Tang appears about 350 times as Da Yi, and sacrifices to him were particularly grand, with banquets, drums to announce the rite and offerings of oxen. In the classical canon he is usually named with Emperor Yao, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shun-emperor">Emperor Shun</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wu-of-zhou">King Wu of Zhou</a> as a good ruler, in contrast to Jie and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/di-xin">Di Xin</a>, and the Three Character Classic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> pairs him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yu-the-great">Yu the Great</a> and King Wu as the Three Kings.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tang of Shang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to traditional histories compiled more than a millennium later, above all the Shiji of Sima Qian, the Shang dynasty was founded when <b>Tang of Shang</b>, a lord of the eastern plain, overthrew Jie, the tyrannical last ruler of the Xia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shang-dynasty">Shang dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Another tradition held that most of it was written by <b>Tang of Shang</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/i-ching">I Ching · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
